Herbivorous beetles are among the most diverse animal groups. While this enormous diversity has partly been explained by the rise of the angiosperms (Farrell, 1998; Barraclough et al., 1998) it remains unclear whether herbivorous beetles have speciated in a sympatric or an allopatric mode. Insights into the mode of speciation in herbivorous beetles will contribute to the debate on the generality of sympatric speciation. One of the leaf beetle genera that is the subject of research into sympatric speciation by host race formation is the genus Galerucella.
The wide variety of distantly related but commonly co-occurring host plants and the observation of closely related host-associated Galerucella taxa occurring in the same habitat make Galerucella an interesting genus for studying host switching, with possible consequences for sympatric ecological speciation (Hippa and Koponen, 1986; Nokkala and Nokkala, 1998; Pappers et al., 2001, 2002a; Ilkenen et al., 2003). Whether sympatric speciation by host switching has contributed to the current diversity of Galerucella can only be decided after the phylogenetic relationships between the members of the genus have been resolved (Barraclough and Nee, 2001). We can then assess whether taxa associated with the same host are indeed most closely related to each other and which host switches have taken place.
The systematics of species in the genus Galerucella has traditionally been based on morphological characteristics. However, the large geographical ranges of the species allow much local variation, which has resulted in different species descriptions in different parts of their range, yielding many synonyms for a single species (see for example Reid (2001)). Moreover, several authors believe that leaf beetles adapt and specialize to a particular host plant (monophagy) or several related host plants (oligophagy) (e.g. Novotny et al., 2002) and treat the host-associated taxa as monophagous or oligophagous species (Lohse, 1989; Kangas, 1991). However, since taxa associated with different hosts are able to produce viable and fertile offspring (Hippa and Koponen, 1986; Pappers et al., 2002b), both Ilkenen et al. (2003) and Pappers et al. (2002b) favored a subdivision into host races rather than species, based on the biological species concept (Mayr, 1942).
This study presents a molecular taxonomy of the genus Galerucella, including Palearctic, Nearctic, African and Australian species. The mtDNA characteristics allowed us to construct a phylogenetic tree of Galerucella and test whether typological species are monophyletic. In addition, the phylogenetic tree allowed us to infer the evolutionary relationships between the host-associated taxa within the Galerucella nymphaeae species complex. Finally, we looked at the distribution of host plant use over the phylogenetic tree of Galerucella to find indications for evolutionary relationships.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Sampling, taxa and host plant association
The taxonomy of Galerucella taxonomy has been revised several times (see Silfverberg, 1974). Most Galerucella species that have been described are from the Palearctic region. Wilcox (1971) recognizes 3 North-American Galerucella species, 2 of which were included in our study, along with 8 of the 9 West-Palearctic Galerucella species recognized by Silfverberg (1974) and 1 Galerucella species from Africa. Of the total of 10 Galerucella species included in this study, 5 belong to the subgenus Galerucella and 5 to the subgenus Neogalerucella, (Silfverberg, 1974). Each species was represented by 1 or more individuals. The host-associated groups of G. nymphaeae were treated as G. nymphaeae species complex.
Tricholochmaea decora and Lochmaea suturalis are two species closely related to the genus Galerucella (as reported in Borghuis et al. (unpublished manuscript), based on a partial CO-I sequence from 45 species of the Galerucini subtribe). We therefore used these species to root our phylogeny of Galerucella.
The species in the genus Galerucella feed on host plants belonging to several families. Table 1 shows the Galerucella taxa and outgroup species we studied, their host plant associations and their geographical origins.
## 2.2. DNA extraction, PCR and sequencing
Total genomic DNA was extracted by grinding up the heads and thoraxes of individual dried, fresh, frozen or alcohol-preserved specimens. Abdomens were kept as voucher material. The alcohol in alcohol-preserved specimens was allowed to evaporate at room temperature before grinding. \(500\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\,\,5\%\) CHELEX (Biorad) solution and \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\,\,20\,\mathrm{mg/mL}\) Proteinase-K were added to the ground up individuals and incubated at \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) . Samples were subsequently heated to \(95\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) , to inactivate Proteinase-K.
Mitochondrial gene fragments were obtained using the following PCR primers: a modified Tm-J-206 (5'-GCTAADYAAGCTAT TGGGTTCAT-3') and a newly developed primer N2-N-670 (5'-TTGGGGCTAATTTTTGTCA-3') following the nomenclature by Simon et al. (1994) were used to amplify approximately 465 bp of the NADH-2 gene, while the primers C1-J-2164 and TL2-N-3014 (Simon et al., 1994) were used to amplify approximately 850 bp of the CO-I gene for a subset of the taxa, as listed in Table 1. PCRs were carried out in \(25\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) with \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) dNTPs, \(3\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\) , \(0.4\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) primer and \(1\,\mathrm{U}\) ABgene Taq polymerase, using the following PCR profile: \(4\,\mathrm{min}\) at \(95\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) followed by 20 cycles of denaturation for \(30\,s\) at \(95\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , annealing at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) dropping \(0.5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\) cycle for 1 min and extension at \(72\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1.5\,\mathrm{min}\) , with a final extension step at \(72\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(7\,\mathrm{min}\) . PCR products were visually checked using agarose gel electrophoresis, and purified using the CFX PCR DNA and Gel Band Purification Kit (Amersham Biosciences) according to the manufacturer's instructions, and eluted in \(20\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\,\mathrm{d}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . Purified PCR products were used for direct sequencing with the original PCR primers and with internal primers. Internal primers for the NADH-2 gene were N2-J-231 (5'-CCCACTTATAAGATTACTC-3') and N2-N-534 (5'-CAGTAATCTGAATTTTGG-3'); internal primers for the CO-I gene were C1-J-2577 (5'-ATGTTCTTTCAATAGGRG CAG-3') and C1-J-2626 (5'-ATGATTCCCTTATTTTACAG-3') C1-N-2757 (5'-AAATGTTGNGGRAAAAATGTTA-3'), according to the nomenclature by Simon et al. (1994). Sequence PCRs were performed using the CEQ 2000 DTCS sequencing kit (Beckman Coulter) following the manufacturer's instructions. Sequences were run on a Beckman 8000 sequence analyzer.
## 2.3. Sequence analysis
Two to four sequence chromatograms from each gene fragment were used to construct a consensus sequence for each individual. Both gene regions were aligned using CLUSTAL_X (Thompson et al., 1997) using the default options. Neither of the mitochondrial gene fragments had insertions or deletions in the coding region, making alignment unambiguous. Polymorphisms were double-checked on the chromatograms. Sequence data were submitted to GenBank. GenBank accession numbers of the amplified NADH-2 and CO-I fragments are shown in Table 1.
## 2.4. Phylogenetic analysis
Bayesian and maximum likelihood (ML) phylogenetic analyses were performed with MRBAYES v3.1.2 (Ronquist and Huelsenbeck, 2003) and Maximum Parsimony (MP) phylogenetic analyses with PAUP v.4.0b10 (Swofford, 2002). Model settings for the Bayesian and maximum likelihood analyses were determined using MODELTEST v.3.7 (Posada and Crandall, 1998). This program compares 56 possible models of sequence evolution and selects the model identified as optimal by the Akaike Information Criterion (Akaike,
MODELTEST \((\mathrm{HKY}+\mathrm{I}+\Gamma\) and \(\mathrm{CTR}+\mathrm{I}+\Gamma_{4}\), respectively). The analysis was repeated using constraints on groups of beetle specimens that utilize host plant species from the same family (see Table 1 for host plant associations). In this analysis, the beetle specimens that utilize the same host family were forced into groups of common descent. No other topological constraints were enforced. The shortest tree and the constraint tree were tested for significant difference using the same test as above. A significant difference would indicate that the speciation process in the genus Galerucella was not constrained by host family use, which would indicate that host switching has contributed significantly to speciation.
## 3. Results
## 3.1. Phylogenetic analysis
The Bayesian, ML and MP trees constructed with the NADH-2 dataset and with the combined NADH-2/CO-I dataset are shown in Figs. 1 and 2. The NADH-2 phylogenetic tree (Fig. 1) shows a high level of concordance with the morphological groups described by Silfverberg (1974). The Galerucella species are all located in clade B, unlike the outgroup species L. suturalis and T. decora, which were recovered in clade A. Within the genus Galerucella, the subgenus Galerucella, with the exception of G. grisescens and G. funesta, was recovered in clade C, and the subgenus Neogalerucella in clade D. Finally, within the subgenus Neogalerucella, the lineola species group was distinct from the other species, which were placed in the tenella species group (clades E and F, respectively).
There were two examples of disagreement with Silfverberg's classification. Firstly, Galerucella calmariensis and Galerucella pusilla formed an unresolved cluster and could not be distinguished as monophyletic species based on the mtDNA dataset. Secondly, G. grisescens and G. funesta, which were placed in the subgenus Galerucella according to morphological characteristics, were placed in the Neogalerucella clade according to the mtDNA characteristics. All other species for which we sampled multiple localities and individuals formed monophyletic clades in the phylogenetic tree and were grouped according to Silverberg's classification.
We used phylogenetic constraints on morphological groups, following Silfverberg's classification, to test whether the morphological groups listed in Table 1 were supported by the molecular data. Although G. grisescens and G. funesta from the subgenus Galerucella were placed in the subgenus Neogalerucella (Fig. 1), the p-values for the Kishino–Hasegawa and Shimodaira–Hasegawa tests, 0.200 and 0.452, respectively (Table 2), show that the best ML tree without constraints was not significantly different from the best tree using constraint on the morphological groups.
## 3.2. Host plant use and phylogenetic tree
The genetic diversity between the Galerucella groups and the outgroup, as estimated by the ML model, ranged from 9.7 to
The genetic diversity between the Galerucella groups and the outgroup, as estimated by the ML model, ranged from 9.7 to \(13.1\%\). Using a standard mitochondrial insect molecular clock of \(2\%\) pairwise sequence divergence, these values correspond to an origin of the ancestor of Galerucella around 5–7 Mya. which is much more recent than the divergence of the host plant families. The age of Nymphaeaceae, for example, is estimated to be over 100 Mya and the Rosales, the lineage to which the Rosaceae belong, is estimated to be around 90 my old (Wikström et al., 2001).
G. calmariensis and G. pusilla utilize the same host plant species. The phylogenetic tree shows that they are sister species. However, the pairwise sequence divergence showed very little intra- and inter-species divergence between haplotypes. The phylogenetic relationships within this cluster could therefore not be resolved. In contrast, both Galerucella lineola and G. nymphaeae species complexes showed intra-species diversity in haplotypes. Pairwise sequence divergences were \(2.4\%\) in the G. lineola species complex and \(1.6\%\) in the G. nymphaeae species complex. Although two clusters were recovered in the phylogenetic tree, the host association in the G. nymphaeae species complex was not recovered as a monophyletic clade (Fig. 2).
The analysis of host family use revealed that the best ML tree without constraints on host family was significantly better than the best ML tree using constraints for both the NADH-2 dataset and the combined NADH-2/CO-I dataset. The \(p\)-values of the Kishino–Hasegawa and Shimodaira–Hasegawa tests in both analyses were \(<0.001\).
## 4. Discussion
## 4.1. Phylogenetic relationships: agreement between molecular and morphological systematics
This study attempted to clarify the molecular phylogenetics of part of the Galerucella genus. The results show a high level of agreement with the classical systematics of the genus (Silfverberg, 1974). Nokkala and Nokkala (2004), who used 720 bp of the CO-I mtDNA gene for their phylogenetic inferences on Finnish Galerucella, showed that the subgenus Galerucella, represented by the G. nymphaeae species complex, formed a monophyletic clade. In their study, however, the relationship between G. lineola and the taxa L. suturalis and L. caprea was supported only by low bootstrap values and therefore unclear. Nor did they investigate the relationship with other Galerucella species and the North-American Neogalerucella species. Our study shows that, in addition to the behavioral and morphological characters supporting a distinction between the Neogalerucella and Galerucella species, many molecular traits differentiate between these groups but also differentiate the Galerucella species from the outgroup taxa. The molecular data we obtained also show many characteristics that differentiate between taxonomic levels such as Galerucella species and species groups (following Silfverberg, 1974). All species complexes and all species except one species pair form monophyletic clades.
The phylogenetic relationships within Neogalerucella validate the distinction made by Silfverberg (1974) between the lineola and tenella species groups. In the molecular phylogeny they are represented by distinct clades.
While there is generally high agreement between the molecular and morphological phylogenies, there are also some disagreements. For instance, morphological and molecular data disagree as to the subgenus to which G. funesta and G. grisescens belong. In contrast, G. calmariensis and G. pusilla exhibit many different morphological and life history characteristics and have distinct karyotypes (Nokkala and Nokkala, 1987), although they do not show any mitochondrial differentiation. Similar observations are made in cases of recent speciation with strong selection on parental post-zygotic isolation mechanisms (Arr, 2005).
4.2. Phylogenetic relationships related to host plant use
Our study shows a polyphyletic tree topology for host association (Fig. 2) within the G. nymphaeae species complex. In addition to the haplotypes detected by Nokkala and Nokkala (2004), we observed other haplotypes in the G. nymphaeae species complex. In contrast to the findings of the Finnish study, this resulted in a polyphyletic tree topology for host association. Within the G. nymphaeae species complex, the individuals from Europe were divided into two haplotype clades, both including all host associations. The North-American G. nymphaeae specimen are more closely related to one of the European clades than the European clades are to each other.
