There are three explanations for the lack of correlation between host association and mitochondrial haplotype. The first would be that the host-associated taxa do not represent biological species
and continue to exchange genes and cytoplasmic organelles such as mitochondria. Secondly, the host-associated taxa may represent separate species, but occasional hybridization events have resulted in introgression of foreign mitochondria that spread through the population. Thirdly, the host-associated taxa have only recently evolved into separate species and incomplete lineage sorting is shown as a lack of coalescence.
Previous research has shown that the host-associated taxa of G. nymphaeae are morphologically and genetically differentiated, but are able to produce viable and fertile offspring (Hippa and Koponen, 1986; Pappers et al., 2002b). These studies hypothesized that these host associations have evolved recently and in sympatry. The morphological, behavioral and life history characteristics that differentiate between the host-associated groups may have been subjected to different evolutionary forces compared to neutral genetic characteristics such as mtDNA (see for example Merila and Crnokrak, 2001). We conclude that there is support for a recent diversification process, in view of the differentiation in morphology, life history and behavior of the host-associated taxa and the lack of it in their mtDNA. Moreover, the large pairwise sequence divergence between the haplotype clusters within the G. nymphaeae species complex indicates that the intra-species haplotype divergence is relatively old. Strong host preference and the genetic differences found in other studies (Hippa and Koponen, 1986; Pappers et al., 2002a) are both clear indicators of low levels of gene flow. We therefore believe that the host-associated taxa, whether they are species or host races, have evolved more recently than the haplotype clusters.
G. calmariensis and G. pusilla are the only species that could not be resolved as monophyletic clades. Unlike the species complexes described above, haplotype diversity within the pusilla–calmariensis clade is very small. These species exhibit distinct karyotypes with different chromosome numbers (Nokkala and Nokkala, 1987). Therefore, hybridization between these species seems unlikely. These distinct karyotypes imply that the small haplotype diversity is more likely to be explained by the process of recent speciation, with incomplete lineage sorting at the mitochondrial level, than by recent introgression and incomplete isolation. This is supported by the observation that the taxa in the tenella group (Galerucella tenella, G. calmariensis and G. pusilla) are differentiated by very few molecular characteristics, with a maximum of \(2\%\) sequence divergence. In addition, both G. calmariensis and G. pusilla feed on the same host plant, whereas the G. lineola and G. nymphaeae species groups use several unrelated host plants. Thus, the evolution towards pre- and post-zygotic reproductive isolation within the calmariensis–pusilla group on the one hand and the nymphaeae and lineola species groups on the other, might have been the result of different selective pressures, in which the shared use of the host plant Lythrum salicaria may have resulted in the rapid development of post-zygotic reproductive isolation between G. pusilla and G. calmariensis. The lack of lineage sorting between G. pusilla and G. calmariensis despite their reproductive isolation, shows that speciation in the host-associated groups of the G. nymphaeae species complex may have progressed further than one would conclude from molecular evidence.
