## 5. Conclusion
Our results show a high level of agreement with the classical systematics of the genus Galerucella, but the phylogenetic signal is not strong enough to resolve relationships between very closely related taxa. The species complex as a whole is defined as a distinct gene pool under the phylogenetic species concept (PSC), however, the host-associated taxa of the G. nymphaeae species complex are not.
Regardless of the geographic mode of speciation, the results presented here suggest that host switching contributes to the formation of taxa in Galerucella. This is based on the finding of a relatively recent divergence of the host-associated taxa of G. nymphaeae as well as the observation that many sister taxa in Galerucella utilize different host plant families.
The evidence suggests that host switching did occur, but further evidence such as historical and present day gene flow is required for the sympatric speciation mode proposed by Bush (1975): Historical gene flow because the speciating groups must be able to exchange genes during the process of sympatric speciation and present day gene flow is required to determine the level of reproductive isolation.
Other well-known organisms for the study of sympatric speciation by host switching, such as Rhagoletis pomonella (Feder et al., 1988) and the pea aphid (Acyrthosiphon pisum) (Hawthorne and Via, 2001), have switched to introduced hosts and have diversified into sympatric host races in the last century. The G. nymphaeae species complex uses multiple non-introduced co-existing host species. Evidence for sympatric speciation in the G. nymphaeae species complex will, therefore, add to the debate on the generality of sympatric speciation by host switching.
